label Choice D2.2-A311
You decide to issue a second challenge.\
\
You “Who goes there!?”
To add emphasis, you grab the bolt of your Lee Enfield rifle and chamber a round.
Something flies over the top of the parapet and lands into the trench near your feet. A German Stick Grenade! It explodes before you can react, killing you instantly.
set {chosen_path} = "Death8"
[end_timeline]
label Choice D2.2-A312
Convinced that someone is on the other side of the trench, you grab a grenade, pull the pin, and toss it over the parapet. Despite the proximity, the sandbags and trench wall are sufficient to absorb the blast. The grenade hits the earth.
Voice\: “Handgranate! Deckung! Deckung!”
The panic voices of German soldiers are silenced by the explosion of your hand grenade. To your side Cpl Barron, the sentry, another soldier and, to your surprise, Sergeant Fitzgerald, the Platoon Sergeant, arrive, rifles at the ready, except for Fitzgerald and Barron, who are both holding grenades.
They toss their grenades over the parapet. The explosions are met with silence.
Sgt Fitzgerald “Sentry, use the periscope and confirm we got ‘em.”
A soldier walks up to the firestep and scans the area with a Trench Periscope, an optical device that looks like a long tube, allowing the sentry to scan no man’s land while remaining inside the trench.
At the same time, Second Lieutenant Holmes, the Platoon Commander arrives with a Flare Gun in hand.
2Lt Holmes\: “Allow me to illuminate the area, sentry.”\
\
You hear a distinct ‘PLOOP’ as the projectile launches into the air with a ‘SWOOSH’, detonating in the sky with a muffled bang.
Instantly, the area in front of your section of trench becomes brightly illuminated for the sentry. \
\
Sentry\: “I see three dead Germans, Sir.”
A large and grim looking NCO bumps your shoulder. He is nearly thirty years old and was Mentioned-in-Dispatches for saving a wounded soldier in No Man’s Land.
Sgt Fitzgerald\: “What were you thinking sending the sentry - you should have sent someone else and kept eyes on No Man’s Land.”
You realize now your mistake. You were lucky that nobody got hurt or killed as a result. You imagine you might get written up for the mistake.
set {chosen_path} = "3.1A"
[end_timeline]
label Choice D2.2-A32
You peer over the parapet and scan the muddy, crater-filled landscape. Once a farmer’s field used for growing either wheat, sugar beets, or potatoes, it’s now a perilous hellscape full of unexploded shells, barbed wire, and the bodies of dead soldiers in varying states of decay.
Somewhere in the distance, approximately a hundred meters from you, the enemy awaits in their trench. You wonder if a German soldier is also looking into No Man’s Land. Suddenly, you notice what appears to be the silhouette of a helmet in the distance.
Before you can react, you see the muzzle flash of a sniper’s rifle… it’s the last thing you ever see. The bullet strikes your head. \
\
You Died.
set {chosen_path} = "Death9"
[end_timeline]
label Choice D2.2-A33
You stand your ground, not daring to move to the fire step. Slowly, you reach down to your left hip where, between some ammunition pouches, you find the hilt of your bayonet and pull it out of its frog.
A 17 inch blade is an intimidating looking weapon, especially when it’s attached to the muzzle of your Lee-Enfield. The bayonet clicks securely into place.
You then work the rifle’s action, and as quietly as possible, chamber a round. Bringing the rifle up to your shoulder, you focus the sights on the top of the parapet, hoping that the sentry returns any second now.
To your dismay, you notice a German helmet slowly rise above the parapet as an enemy soldier risks a peek into the trench. Unfortunate for him, his head popped up right in front of your rifle. You fire a round, striking the soldier in the head.
Shouts in German erupt as you load another round. Two more soldiers appear above the parapet - you fire and take down one but the other, armed with a pistol, manages to fire a shot, striking you in the chest.
The round hits you like a speeding train, sending you flying back against the trench wall. To your surprise, the pain is dulled - it was too quick for your mind to register.
The German readies his weapon to fire another and you close your eyes, awaiting the inevitable.
BANG, BANG, BANG!
You recognized the sound - it came from Lee Enfields\: three to be precise. Eyes opening, you see the Sentry, Cpl Barron, and Sergeant Fitzgerald, the Platoon Sergeant, with rifles raised.
Moments later flares explode above No Man’s Land followed by machine gun fire from your side of the trench. A pain unlike any you’ve ever experienced sends electricity through your body as your nerves begin to register the damage.
Sgt Fitzgerald comes to your side, retrieves the wound dressing you have stitched on the inside bottom flap of your tunic and presses it hard against your chest, which makes you groan aloud. Your vision blurs from the pain.
Sgt Fitzgerald “Stay with me Private, your going to be okay. Get some stretcher bearers here on the double!
But his voice fades into a distant, muffled echo as you slip into unconsciousness.
You awaken sometime later in a hospital. You try to move but a jap of pain in your chest makes you reconsider and you settle back into the bed. A woman in a blue uniform comes into view - a nursing sister. 
Nurse “Easy, don’t move. You just had surgery.”\
\
You glance to your right and notice your shoulder and arm in a cast.
Nurse “You were lucky the bullet missed your lungs but it did a number to your shoulder. You won’t be using your arm or lifting anything soon.”
The damage from your wound is enough to have you invalided home. Your time serving in the Great War is now finished.
Eventually, your wound heals although your right arm never quite feels the same and your shoulder becomes a constant source of pain for the rest of your days. Still, you count yourself fortunate to have survived the war when so many of your friends and comrades did not.
set {chosen_path} = "End1"
[end_timeline]
label A3: Go on Senty Duty
You continue your sentry duty, using a Trench Periscope to safely peer into No Man’s Land. Your eyes scan the muddy, crater-filled landscape. Once a farmer’s field used for growing either wheat, sugar beets, or potatoes, it’s now a perilous hellscape full of unexploded shells, barbed wire, and the bodies of dead soldiers in varying states of decay.
Somewhere in the distance, approximately a hundred meters from you, the enemy awaits in their trench. You wonder if a German soldier is also looking into No Man’s Land.
Suddenly, you notice a few silhouettes, possibly people. You focus on the shapes and then, to your dismay, notice a spark - did someone just light a cigarette in No Man’s Land?
You have trouble believing what you think you just saw\: nobody would ever be that reckless. Still, you’re having trouble trying to make sense of it. Perhaps the lenses of the periscope need to be cleaned?
