Feeling compelled to help, you climb out of the sap and run to the injured man. Your legs feel wobbly and your stomach sinks like you’re in freefall. The fear is beyond anything you’ve ever felt and you vaguely register the bullets striking the earth around you and mortar rounds exploding. A persistent thought sticks in the back of your mind - I’m going to die - but you push past it, focusing instead on the man on the ground. Reaching out with numb fingers, you grasp the soldier’s webbing and desperately drag him back to the sap.
