Brad\: Well, in a couple of days we’ll be back in the reserve line and then it’s a week of rest.
You\: *Sigh* Sometimes it feels like this war is never going to end.
set {chosen_path} = "3.1AMM1"
[end_timeline]
label 3.1AMM1
You can’t recall falling asleep. The next morning you awake, get your gear back on, and head out of the dugout for stand to, followed by the morning’s inspection. When 2Lt Holmes comes up to you he’s smiling.
2Lt Holmes\: You’re out of uniform, Lance Corporal.\
\
You\: Sir?\
\
He extends his right hand and you grasp it, his left hand producing a large, single wool chevron.
2Lt Holmes\: Long overdue… Also, I’ve put you in for a decoration. Sergeant Fitzgerald told me about what you did yesterday. Congratulations. Keep it up and you’ll be a section commander soon enough.
You\: Thank-you, Sir!\
\
Sgt Fitzgerald walks up next and gives you his characteristic smack on the arm that nearly knocks you over. He’s beaming with pride.
Once everyone is dismissed, Cpl Barron takes you aside.\
\
Cpl Barron\: Well, you’re now Section 2IC. That means you’ll be helping me do the inspections and supervising work details.
- I have some questions
	set {chosen_path} = "3.1B"
- No questions
	set {chosen_path} = "3.2"
[end_timeline]
label 3.1B
Cpl Barron\: Yes, Lance Corporal?
- Can you explain my duties again?
	set {chosen_path} = "3.1C"
- Any advice?
	set {chosen_path} = "3.1D"
- What privileges do I get?
	set {chosen_path} = "3.1G"
- No questions
	set {chosen_path} = "3.2"
[end_timeline]
label 3.1C
Cpl Barron\: You are in a junior leadership position. It’s your job now to help me run the section. That means ensuring duties are assigned and completed, conducting barrack and dugout inspections, and, if necessary, enforcing discipline.
set {chosen_path} = "3.1B"
[end_timeline]
label 3.1D
Cpl Barron\: When I became a Lance Corporal, I found it hard at first not jumping in to do jobs. Fitzy jacked me up right quick and told me that it was my job to supervise and follow-up, not do the work myself. \
\
You\: But won’t that help get the work done quicker?
Cpl Barron\: That’s what I said. But here’s the problem\: if you do that, then there’s nobody left to oversee the work being done. And that’s when mistakes happen.
set {chosen_path} = "3.1B"
[end_timeline]
label 3.1G
Cpl Barron\: Well, it’s not a lot, but, for starters you get more pay. And you get a better spot in the dug out. But you’ll learn quickly that any privilege comes with increased responsibilities. If anything goes awry, Fitzy will be on us first. Always put the needs of our soldiers first.
set {chosen_path} = "3.1B"
[end_timeline]
label 3.2
Cpl Barron\: Okay, we have several men taking up positions along the front. I want you to keep watch on them. Move around and make sure everyone is on task.
You\: Yes Corporal.
Cpl Barron\: Before you go. Remember that everyone will look to you for guidance. We may not be Senior NCOs or Officers, but we are the ones the men see all the time. Lead by example - stay calm, no matter what. Nothing worse for the men than to see their leader panic.
set {chosen_path} = "3.4"
[end_timeline]
label 3.4
You notice two soldiers chatting with each other and not being diligent about keeping sentry. \
You\: Back to your post. \
\
\
Soldier\: Oh, c’mon Lance Corporal. Cut is some slack.
- Order them
	set {chosen_path} = "3.5"
- Let it go
	set {chosen_path} = "3.6"
[end_timeline]
label 3.5
You\: Back to you post, now! That’s an order.\
\
Both men sigh and go back to the fire step.
A chorus of distant thunderous explosions erupt, followed by loud whistling. The ground around you shakes as artillery shells strike the ground around the trench.
- Take cover
	set {chosen_path} = "3.7"
- Run for the dugout
	set {chosen_path} = "3.8"
[end_timeline]
label 3.7
You\: Barrage, take cover!\
\
You and the two men dive into shelters dug into the side of no man’s land.
The ground shakes and debris falls around you. A shell strikes the top of the trench above the shelter, causing the earth to collapse and bury all three of you. The weight presses down on you, making it impossible to move… and breathe!\
\
You suffocate to death.\
\
The End
set {chosen_path} = "Death 18"
[end_timeline]
label 3.8
You\: Barrage, run for the dug out!\
\
The ground shakes as you run back to the safety of your dug out. Shells land all around, men shouting and making their way to the safety of their designated shelters. When you reach your dugout, you all but leap through the entrance. Brad is already there.
Brad\: Think it’ll be a long one?\
\
You\: Not sure, but usually, the longer they are, the more likely they will attack when it’s done.
The bombardment continues for hours. There’s nothing to do but hunker down and wait. To pass the time you eat some food, play cards with Brad, and pen a letter to your parents back home. A new soldier is having trouble with the constant sound of the bombing. Cpl Barron goes over and chats with him, trying to calm his nerves.
The constant shaking and explosions are nerve wracking. It was almost impossible to sleep and the fear of the ceiling caving in made most feel a bit claustrophobic.
More hours pass and still the bombardment continues unabated.\
\
Then, just before dusk, the bombardment suddenly stops.
set {chosen_path} = "A8"
[end_timeline]
label 3.6
You\: Okay, fine.\
\
As you get ready to leave the sound of whistling artillery rings overhead. Before you can say or do anything, a shell lands in the trench and kills you and the soldiers.
set {chosen_path} = "Death 19"
[end_timeline]
label A8
Cpl Barron shouts for everyone to prepare for battle; a small group of German soldiers are rushing towards your trench.
- Grab your rifle and help Cpl Barron usher folks out to the front lines.
	set {chosen_path} = "A9"
[end_timeline]
label A9
You\: Come on, move!\
\
You help the Cpl usher people out. The entire company is now stationed on the front lines. You notice 2Lt Holmes walking beside Captain Shaw, the Company Commander, with the Company Sergeant Major, Warrant Officer Shepherd on his other side.
Capt Shaw is a tall, well-manucured man with a crisp uniform and mustache. He’s the poster image of an officer, and among the undress ribbons on his uniform, you note one that is white with a purple line in the center, indicating that he’s been awarded a Military Cross. He holds a swagger stick under his left arm.
If Capt Shaw is the quintessential gentleman soldier, WO Shepherd is the textbook image of a Sergeant-Major. While shorter than Sgt Fitizgerald, his strong frame, piercing stare, and chestful of ribbons, makes every Non-Commissioned Member nervous. A career military man, he’s experienced and battle-hardened.
Capt Shaw issues an order to 2Lt Holmes, who in turn orders everyone to “Fix Bayonets”. The CSM immediately supports the order, by barking out commands, which down the line various Platoon Sergeants echo.
Next, calls to “Stand To” ring out. Unlike morning and evening Stand To, you can hear a difference in the tone, a seriousness that lets everyone know this is real. Cpl Barron and you move onto the fire steps along with everyone else, minus the officers and senior NCOs who stay back, watching the lines. Brad is on your left and Cpl Barron on your right. You all step up, point your rifles into No Man’s Land, and scan the area.
Over the parapet, you see a small group of German soldiers rushing, line abreast, across the scarred wasteland. The order to Open Fire is given and you squeeze the trigger. A chorus of ‘bangs’ ringing out as everyone  attacks. Reloading your rifle, you note that the Germans appeared to have stopped their advance, hunkering down in NO Man’s Land. You and Brad trade puzzled looks.
A loud whistle rings out, followed by a loud BOOM as explosions erupt in No Man’s Land between the Germans and your trench. You duck for cover, but then pop back up to see a yellow-brown gas blowing with the wind towards your trench.
As the cloud slowly creeps towards your trench, the voices of your NCOs and Officers begin shouting “GAS GAS GAS!”, confirming what you already suspected - the enemy was launching a chemical attack. You suffered from a Chlorine Gas attack some years ago in Ypres, but this isn’t the same weapon\: its Mustard Gas.
- Put on your gas mask
	set {chosen_path} = "A11"
- Take one more shot at the enemy then put on your gas mask
	set {chosen_path} = "A12"
[end_timeline]
label A11
The procedure to put on your Small Box Respirator (SBR) is a well practiced drill and, at this point, second nature\: you take a deep breath, hold it, open the large canvas pouch at your chest, pull out the jumble of rubberized canvas, round glass eyepieces, and a rubber corrugated tube attached to a tin canister, and place the mask over your head. You then secure it via straps, ensuring it’s tight. The canister, which contains charcoal and asbestos, filters the chemicals, allowing you to breathe.
Checking Brad, you take your rifle and once again look over the parapet. The gas is getting closer but, behind it, you see the silhouettes of the enemy advancing, using the gas as a screen. You fire at the enemy but the gas becomes thicker. Soon you can’t see anything at all beyond the barrier.
- Run Back to Dug Out
	set {chosen_path} = "EDashCharge"
- Stay at your post.
	set {chosen_path} = "A13"
[end_timeline]
label A12
You take aim and fire one last shot, then proceed to put on your Small Box Respirator (SBR). The procedure is second nature to you know\: you take a deep breath, hold it, open the large canvas pouch at your chest, pull out the jumble of rubberized canvas, round glass eyepieces, and a rubber corrugated tube attached to a tin canister, and place the mask over your head. You then secure it via straps, ensuring it’s tight. The canister, which contains charcoal and asbestos, filters the chemicals, allowing you to breathe.
Unfortunately, some of the gas made it to your position before you were able to fully secure your SBR. You start to feel a burning sensation on your face, followed by your hands and neck. Your eyes are very irritated and you’re having trouble seeing anything clearly in  No Man’s Land.
- Take off your mask and rub your eyes.
	set {chosen_path} = "1.12"
- Keep your gas mask on even though your eyes are burning.
	set {chosen_path} = "A13"
[end_timeline]
label 1.12
Removing your mask to rub your eyes, you unwittingly expose your face and lungs to the gas, but because of the delayed symptoms, you replace your mask without realizing the damage you’ve done.
As the hours pass, you begin to suffer from excruciating pain. Blisters appear on your skin and your lungs are damaged. You are sent to a decontamination unit and, soon after, to a field aid station.
Your injuries prove quite extensive - you are sent to a hospital in France and spend the better part of the month recovering. You are invalided back home and honourably discharged from the army.
set {chosen_path} = "Death20"
[end_timeline]
label A13
The Mustard Gas blows over you and into the trench. You can feel a burning sensation over your hands and neck, one that gradually gets more and more irritating. But your continued fire forces the enemy to retreat. \
\
Capt Shaw\: Cease Fire!
Eventually, the order to Stand Down is given. You head to a decontamination unit in the back lines, removing your clothing and washing your body of toxins before changing into a clean uniform and heading back to your dugout. 
set {chosen_path} = "A14"
[end_timeline]
label A14
Your artillery conducts a seven-day barrage on the enemy trench. It’s the precursor to a push - a coordinated attack that will see everyone go Over the Top and charge at the German trench. During this time, you continue to supervise watches as well as help prepare soldiers for the upcoming attack.
About two hours before the bombardment is due to stop at 0500 hours, you head out of your bunker and make your way to the front lines.
[end_timeline]
label A16
A roar emanates from the line as every Canadian, including yourself, let out a battle cry as you pull yourselves over the parapet and step into No Man's Land.
The order to fix bayonets is given. You quickly attach the blade to the end of your Lee Enfield and then feel the soldier to your right poke your arm. You don’t recognize him - he’s new, young, and inexperienced.\
\
Soldier\: Lance Corporal… have you been Over the Top before?
You\: A few times. \
\
Soldier\: I’m… scared. \
\
You force a smile, projecting calm despite also feeling afraid. \
\
You\: That’s normal. Focus on your task, keep your rifle pointed straight, and your training will kick in.
He gives a shaky nod. Just then the artillery stops. Officers behind the fire steps begin blowing on trench whistles.
A roar emanates from the line as every Canadian, including yourself, let out a battle cry as you pull yourselves over the parapet and step into No Man's Land.
You step into the wasteland along with thousands of other Canadians, doing your best to close the distance as quickly as possible. Enemy mortar rounds explode around you, killing soldiers, throwing bodies into the air, and sending shrapnel and debris raining down on you.
Your legs feel wobbly and numb. The ground is also uneven, which makes running even harder, and the constant explosions and gun fire makes things hazy and details hard to see in the distance.
Enemy soldiers defending the line start opening fire, sending bullets like a horizontal rain. Machine gun echos and men scream as metal and heat rip through uniforms, flesh, and bone. Hundreds are dying in a matter of seconds - these may be the last seconds of your life but you push the thought out of your mind.
You're close enough now to make out the shapes of German helmets along the trench.
- Shoot at the German soldiers
