label A16C
A soldier ahead of you dives onto the barbed wire, allowing you to safely run over his back. You jump into the trench and immediately thrust your bayonet into an unsuspecting German soldier.
set {chosen_path} = "A19"
[end_timeline]
label A17
You head to an open space on the firing line. Soon after, the order to fix bayonets is given. You quickly attach the blade to the end of your Lee Enfield.
The artillery stops and your body tenses. Officers behind the fire steps begin blowing on trench whistles.
A roar rings out from the line as every Canadian, including yourself, let out a battle cry as you pull yourselves over the parapet and step into No Man's Land.
You step into the wasteland along with thousands of other Canadians, doing your best to close the distance as quickly as possible. Enemy mortar rounds explode around you, killing soldiers, throwing bodies into the air, and sending shrapnel and debris raining down on you.
Your legs feel wobbly and numb. The ground is also uneven, which makes running even harder, and the constant explosions and gun fire makes things hazy and details hard to see in the distance.
Something catches your eyes in front of you - a wounded Canadian on the ground. It’s your friend Brad; he’s been shot!
- Help him
	set {chosen_path} = "A18"
- Keep moving forward to the enemy trench
	set {chosen_path} = "D14"
[end_timeline]
label A18
Despite the bullets and explosions, you dive down onto the ground beside Brad and check him over. The bullet struck his abdomen, which means there’s a chance he can live if he were to get medical treatment.
Bullets pelt the dirt right beside you, causing your body to become rigid. You feel exposed.\
\
You\: Brad, can you hear me?
Brad\: They shot me…\
\
You\: I know buddy. I’m going to take care of it.
You grab the wound dressing stitched on the inside bottom flap of Brad’s tunic, rip open the canvas bag, and pull out the bandage. He groans in pain as you press it hard against his wound. \
\
Brad\: I wanna go home… I want my mom…
Your mind is racing. In the distance you can just make out the enemy. Seeing your wounded friend brings on a surge of anger. If he dies, you’ll have lost your last friend.
- Pick Brad up and head back to your trench
	set {chosen_path} = "A18A"
- Drag Brad back to your trench
	set {chosen_path} = "D12"
- Fight the Germans
	set {chosen_path} = "D13"
[end_timeline]
label A18A
Grabbing Brad by his webbing, you lift him up and then, crouching you position him onto your shoulders and lift him off the ground.
Adrenaline releasing, you run back to your lines, zig-zagging left and right to make yourself a harder target. You hope the sight of carrying a wounded soldier might just make the German soldiers reconsider shooting at you.
Approaching your trench you jump over the parapet and crash into the duck boards below, allowing your body to absorb the impact. It’s a miracle that you didn’t break both of your legs.
An officer approaches you and, seeing that you’ve brought a wounded soldier, immediately call for aid. Meanwhile, you roll Brad onto his back and lay beside him, trying to catch your breath.
Brad\: Thanks for getting me out of there buddy. I didn’t want the rats to get me.\
\
You\: Nothing is going to get you. We’re getting help and you’ll be going back home.
Brad’s demeanor changes - he becomes calm. His breathing slows and placing a hand upon yours, he looks at you with knowing eyes.
Brad\: Write to my parents. Tell them I love them… tell my Mom I’m sorry I broke my promise. That I didn’t come home. \
\
You\: Brad, stop it! The doctor will take care of you, you can’t…
He squeezes your hand with the last of his strength - you realize you can’t ignore the truth.\
\
Brad\: Promise me.\
\
The tears flow freely from your eyes.
You\: … I promise Brad.\
\
He smiles and closes his eyes. His body becomes heavier in your arms, his grip loosening. Men come to your side with a stretcher, but it's too late. Brad is gone.
Eventually, you are joined by a wounded 2Lt Holmes and Sgt Fitzgerald. Unfortunately, Cpl Barron was killed in the attack.
The next day, you are promoted to the rank of Corporal and made Section Commander.
You serve until the end of the war, rising to the rank of Sergeant. Of your eight friends, you alone have survived The Great War. You carry the memory of your fallen comrades for the rest of your days, but honour their memory and live life to the fullest. You get married, have a family, and name your three sons\: Jack, Bill, and Brad.
Many years later, you stand in a well kept graveyard before rows of white-grey gravestones, wearing a navy blue blazer, grey trousers, medals gleaming in the early morning sun. Your family - children and grandchildren - are with you.
set {chosen_path} = "End 4"
[end_timeline]
label D12
You grip both straps of Brad’s webbing and proceed to drag him back towards your trench. You ignore the bullets striking the earth around you, pulling your wounded friend across the wasteland as quickly as you can. Suddenly, you feel a sharp pain in your side, followed by another beside it.
Pushing the pain aside, you continue pulling your friend closer and closer to your trench. Finally, with one last heave, you and Brad tumble into the trench.
Stretchers arrive and you are both taken to an aid station. While your actions managed to save your friend’s life, you succumb to your wounds. Your heroism earns you a posthumous Order of St. John Life Saving Medal in Gold.
set {chosen_path} = "End 5"
[end_timeline]
label D13
You take up a prone position beside your wounded friend and aim your rifle at a distant German helmet. The explosions, bullets, and screams make your body turn to ice. Your limbs are numb and - not for the first time - you lose control of your bowls.
You fire your weapon, work the bolt, camber another round, and fire again. Are your shots doing anything? You can’t tell. 
Machine gun fire suddenly sprays overhead, dropping dozens of soldiers. Before you can move, another spray of bullets strikes the earth around you, this time killing both you and Brad.
Your bodies are but two of thousands littering a small segment of land, soon to be feasted upon by rats. Your remains are never recovered.\
\
The End
set {chosen_path} = "Death 25"
[end_timeline]
label D14
You feel bad leaving Brad behind but it would be almost suicidal to stop in the middle of an advance, with little cover. You figure attacking and keeping any enemy fire away from him is the best move.
You rush the enemy trench along with your comrades, firing your rifle. Suddenly, a machine gun housed in a protective Pill Box behind the enemy trench, opens fire. Bullets spray across you and your fellow soldiers, killing you all in seconds.
set {chosen_path} = "Death 26"
[end_timeline]
label A19
Pulling your blade from the dead soldier, you glance at two other Germans and feel a sudden chill run through you - the fear of imminent death - but neither of the men move. They are terrified, frozen in place.
You consider taking them prisoner, or at least you would have if two other soldiers hadn’t shot them as they jumped into the trench. There’s no time to dwell on it – you have to keep moving.
Part of the trench ahead has collapsed from an artillery shell, blocking the way. Machine gun fire draws your attention to a spot behind the enemy trench. There you noted a large concrete fortification. 
Nicknamed a Pill Box, it is elevated above the trench, which not only provides the machine gun crew a perfect vantage point to fire on enemy soldiers, but also protection from enemy fire.
- Head back into No Man’s Land to find a way around the collapsed trench
	set {chosen_path} = "D191"
- Make your way to the pillbox
	set {chosen_path} = "A19A"
[end_timeline]
label D191
You decide to climb back out of the trench and slither around on the battlefield. Pulling yourself over the enemy parapet, you crouch down and move sideways along the edge of the trench. 
You feel something hold you back, gripping your uniform and webbing. It’s the enemy barbed wire. You struggle to free yourself but, as you do, machine gun fire strikes you. You die, your body propped up by the wire.
set {chosen_path} = "Death 27"
[end_timeline]
label A19A
All around you, Canadians and Germans are locked in close quarter combat. An enemy soldier rounds the corner of the communication trench, and you fire a round into him. Working the bolt, a second German appears and swings at you with a trench shovel. 
There now appears to be more Canadians in the trench now – the Germans have retreated.
You lean back, narrowly avoiding the strike. You hear a TING as the metal shovel brushes the front of your steel brodie helmet.
You fall back and onto the ground. The German soldier immediately raises his shovel over his head, but as he brings it down to strike, you manage to raise your rifle and stab him with your bayonet.
Before you can recover, three more Germans appear. One, an officer, is holding a pistol. He takes aim at you.
BANG.
You blink and see the officer fall back. 2Lt Holmes is standing, revolver in hand. He immediately fires at another German soldier - at the same time, a shot from behind you drops the third.
2Lt Holmes\: Help him up.\
\
Strong hands grab you under your armpits and lift you to your feet. You turn to see Sgt Fitzgerald.
Sgt Fitzgerald\: You alright, lad?\
\
You\: Yes Sergeant. Thanks.
There appears to be even more Canadians around then Germans now.\
\
2Lt Holmes\: We have this part of the trench, keep moving!
He and Sgt. Fitzgerald turn and head in the opposite direction as you move up the communication line with some other soldiers, intent on making it to the pill box.
You reach a part of the enemy trench that leads up to the pill box. Thankfully, you are not directly in front of it.
- Throw a grenade into the pill box
	set {chosen_path} = "A20"
- Approach the pill box and take it by shooting the soldiers inside
	set {chosen_path} = "D16"
[end_timeline]
label A20
Sliding out of the trench on your stomach, you military crawl towards the concrete fortification. You’re now close enough to feel the rattling of the machine gun fire and hear the German soldiers inside shouting orders.
You roll onto your back, pull another grenade from a pouch, a No. 5 Mk I "Mills Bomb". You grasp the pineapple shaped grenade and hold down the lever, keeping it in place. With some effort, you pull the pin out.
Then, with eyes fixed on the rectangular shaped opening of the pill box, you release the lever, allowing it to spring free, but hold onto the grenade to cook it.
One Second... Two Seconds
You toss it towards the opening at the exact time the machine gun begins to fire. The sound of the gun may have muffled the bomb’s landing. Or perhaps the Germans noticed it. Either way, the grenade explodes almost immediately, killing all inside. The gun falls silent.
You move towards the entrance of the box, rifle ready, and enter. You first confirm that each soldier is dead before searching the area. Looking out of the pill box opening you notice some German soldiers making their way into the communication trench.
Turning, you look back and, to your surprise, see a British Lewis Gun on the cold concrete floor, beside another machine gun you don’t recognize. Perhaps they were captured at some point?
